#!/bin/bash
# A second pair of eyes on what a change looks like: Gemini 4 Neon at xhigh in
# the Antigravity CLI is shown the pictures and says what a careful designer
# would catch that the layout lint and `tsession review` can't: a line set
# right under another, uneven gaps, edges and baselines not lined up, text cut
# or crowded, something out of keeping with the rest of the window.
#
#   tools/dev/agy-look [--about "WHAT CHANGED"] PICTURE.png…
#
# PICTUREs: the change's pictures (headless: tools/dev/tsession shot),
# each state and scale worth seeing, in a folder of yours under /var/tmp.
# It prints its findings and writes them to agy-look.md beside the first
# picture (what it did, to agy-look.log). Check each finding against the
# picture yourself, and fix what's real. Beside tools/dev/opus-look
# (Claude Opus 5.5 at xhigh for visual design) and tools/dev/astra-review +
# tools/dev/agy-review (for code): changes with visual design work use all
# three reviewers.
#
# The look is an agy in a tmux session, as every agent here is
# (`tools/dev/agents look`: this is that), so it can be read while it
# looks (`tools/dev/agents peek agy-FOLDER`), answered if it asks
# anything, and told more (`agents say`). Run this in the background: it
# prints a line a minute of what the look is doing, and returns when the
# review is written (0), or as soon as the look needs you (3: answer it,
# then run this again with the same pictures to go on waiting). It works
# from the pictures' folder, not the repository, and no command of its
# goes ahead unasked.
exec /usr/bin/python3 "$(dirname "$0")/agents" look "$@"
